{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PPXDUz43q1SXCDZKZeEBEh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions — Ticket abcd-5\n2\t\n3\tADR-style decisions evaluating intake's detected stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`) against the FR/NFR list. This is a frontend-only, single-page React app with no backend — every decision below stays inside that boundary.\n4\t\n5\t## ADR-1: Keep React 19 + Vite, no framework change\n6\t\n7\t**Context:** Intake detected React ^19.2.8 + Vite ^8.2.0, already scaffolded, PRD explicitly pins this stack and states \"no backend.\"\n8\t\n9\t**Decision:** Accept the detected stack as-is. All ten FRs (FR-1..FR-10) are UI/state features layered onto the existing `HomePage`/`Card`/`Grid`/`EmptyState`/`SearchInput`/`ProfileModal` components — nothing requires SSR, routing, or a different framework.\n10\t\n11\t**Consequences:** No migration cost. Design phase works entirely within `src/components`, `src/pages`, `src/lib`, `src/data`.\n12\t\n13\t## ADR-2: No new npm dependencies — extend existing modules only\n14\t\n15\t**Context:** NFR-3 explicitly forbids new dependencies and requires extending `Grid`, `Card`, `EmptyState`, `SearchInput`, `filterItems` rather than duplicating them.\n16\t\n17\t**Decision:** All new capability (department filter, sort, view toggle, favorites, pagination, stats bar) is built with plain React state/props and edits to the existing components/`filterItems.js`. No state-management library, no UI kit, no date/format library.\n18\t\n19\t**Consequences:** Slightly more manual wiring (e.g. sort comparator, favorites reducer) than a library would give, but zero dependency risk and matches the PRD constraint directly.\n20\t\n21\t## ADR-3: Local component state (useState/useReducer) — no global state library\n22\t\n23\t**Context:** NFR-1 requires synchronous client-side filtering/sorting/pagination over \"at most a few hundred items\" with no debouncing/memoization required. NFR-3 bars new dependencies (rules out Redux/Zustand/etc.), and intake's stack notes confirm \"no router/global state lib.\"\n24\t\n25\t**Decision:** Hold search text, department filter, sort mode, view mode, favorites set, favorites-only toggle, and visible-count (pagination) as React state colocated in `HomePage` (or a single custom hook it owns), derived filtering/sorting done with plain array methods on each render — no memoization layer required at this data scale.\n26\t\n27\t**Consequences:** Re-render cost stays trivial at \"a few hundred items\"; if the dataset ever grows an order of magnitude, revisit with `useMemo`. Must still avoid accidental O(n²) patterns (e.g. re-filtering inside a loop) per NFR-1.\n28\t\n29\t## ADR-4: localStorage for favorites persistence, with in-memory fallback\n30\t\n31\t**Context:** FR-5 requires favorites mirrored to `localStorage` key `team-directory:favorites`; NFR-2 requires a localStorage failure not to crash the page and to degrade to in-memory favorites for that session.\n32\t\n33\t**Decision:** Use the browser `localStorage` API directly (no wrapper library), guarded by try/catch on both read (init) and write (on toggle). No IndexedDB, no backend persistence — matches \"no backend\" architecture.\n34\t\n35\t**Consequences:** Favorites are per-browser, non-syncing, session-durable only when localStorage is available. This is sufficient per FR-5/NFR-2; no further durability requirement stated.\n36\t\n37\t## ADR-5: Plain CSS, existing per-component `.css` file convention\n38\t\n39\t**Context:** Intake detected `cssPreprocessor: css`; existing components (`Card.css`, `Grid.css`, `ProfileModal.css`, `Header.css`) each ship a sibling stylesheet. NFR-5 requires responsive wrapping/collapsing behavior (toolbar wrap, grid column collapse, list single-column).\n40\t\n41\t**Decision:** Implement all new/changed layout (toolbar, stats bar, grid/list view, responsive breakpoints) in plain CSS using the same per-component file pattern, with media queries for NFR-5. No CSS-in-JS, no Sass/Less, no new build tooling.\n42\t\n43\t**Consequences:** Consistent with existing codebase conventions; zero new dependencies.\n44\t\n45\t## ADR-6: Vitest + Testing Library for new logic and accessibility behavior\n46\t\n47\t**Context:** `vitest`, `@testing-library/react`, and `jsdom` are already devDependencies with existing `*.test.jsx` files per component. NFR-4 requires specific a11y behaviors (keyboard operability, aria-labels, focus trap/restoration on `ProfileModal`, live-region `Toast`) that are best verified with interaction tests rather than manual QA.\n48\t\n49\t**Decision:** Extend the existing per-component `*.test.jsx`/`*.test.js` pattern for new behavior (filter/sort/pagination logic in `filterItems.js`, favorites toggle, modal focus management) using the already-installed test stack. No new test runner or a11y-testing library.\n50\t\n51\t**Consequences:** Keeps NFR-3's no-new-dependency constraint intact while giving the design phase a concrete place to specify testable behavior for NFR-4.\n52\t\n53\t---\n54\t\n55\t**Status:** complete\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1351e78-a61e-4705-9c3e-9d357cb70796","uuid":"687973b6-97b5-45bc-8c21-8e87955f2d3a","timestamp":"2026-10-16T16:34:53.346Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/pdlc-forge-new/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions — Ticket abcd-5\n\nADR-style decisions evaluating intake's detected stack (`artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json`) against the FR/NFR list. This is a frontend-only, single-page React app with no backend — every decision below stays inside that boundary.\n\n## ADR-1: Keep React 19 + Vite, no framework change\n\n**Context:** Intake detected React ^19.2.8 + Vite ^8.2.0, already scaffolded, PRD explicitly pins this stack and states \"no backend.\"\n\n**Decision:** Accept the detected stack as-is. All ten FRs (FR-1..FR-10) are UI/state features layered onto the existing `HomePage`/`Card`/`Grid`/`EmptyState`/`SearchInput`/`ProfileModal` components — nothing requires SSR, routing, or a different framework.\n\n**Consequences:** No migration cost. Design phase works entirely within `src/components`, `src/pages`, `src/lib`, `src/data`.\n\n## ADR-2: No new npm dependencies — extend existing modules only\n\n**Context:** NFR-3 explicitly forbids new dependencies and requires extending `Grid`, `Card`, `EmptyState`, `SearchInput`, `filterItems` rather than duplicating them.\n\n**Decision:** All new capability (department filter, sort, view toggle, favorites, pagination, stats bar) is built with plain React state/props and edits to the existing components/`filterItems.js`. No state-management library, no UI kit, no date/format library.\n\n**Consequences:** Slightly more manual wiring (e.g. sort comparator, favorites reducer) than a library would give, but zero dependency risk and matches the PRD constraint directly.\n\n## ADR-3: Local component state (useState/useReducer) — no global state library\n\n**Context:** NFR-1 requires synchronous client-side filtering/sorting/pagination over \"at most a few hundred items\" with no debouncing/memoization required. NFR-3 bars new dependencies (rules out Redux/Zustand/etc.), and intake's stack notes confirm \"no router/global state lib.\"\n\n**Decision:** Hold search text, department filter, sort mode, view mode, favorites set, favorites-only toggle, and visible-count (pagination) as React state colocated in `HomePage` (or a single custom hook it owns), derived filtering/sorting done with plain array methods on each render — no memoization layer required at this data scale.\n\n**Consequences:** Re-render cost stays trivial at \"a few hundred items\"; if the dataset ever grows an order of magnitude, revisit with `useMemo`. Must still avoid accidental O(n²) patterns (e.g. re-filtering inside a loop) per NFR-1.\n\n## ADR-4: localStorage for favorites persistence, with in-memory fallback\n\n**Context:** FR-5 requires favorites mirrored to `localStorage` key `team-directory:favorites`; NFR-2 requires a localStorage failure not to crash the page and to degrade to in-memory favorites for that session.\n\n**Decision:** Use the browser `localStorage` API directly (no wrapper library), guarded by try/catch on both read (init) and write (on toggle). No IndexedDB, no backend persistence — matches \"no backend\" architecture.\n\n**Consequences:** Favorites are per-browser, non-syncing, session-durable only when localStorage is available. This is sufficient per FR-5/NFR-2; no further durability requirement stated.\n\n## ADR-5: Plain CSS, existing per-component `.css` file convention\n\n**Context:** Intake detected `cssPreprocessor: css`; existing components (`Card.css`, `Grid.css`, `ProfileModal.css`, `Header.css`) each ship a sibling stylesheet. NFR-5 requires responsive wrapping/collapsing behavior (toolbar wrap, grid column collapse, list single-column).\n\n**Decision:** Implement all new/changed layout (toolbar, stats bar, grid/list view, responsive breakpoints) in plain CSS using the same per-component file pattern, with media queries for NFR-5. No CSS-in-JS, no Sass/Less, no new build tooling.\n\n**Consequences:** Consistent with existing codebase conventions; zero new dependencies.\n\n## ADR-6: Vitest + Testing Library for new logic and accessibility behavior\n\n**Context:** `vitest`, `@testing-library/react`, and `jsdom` are already devDependencies with existing `*.test.jsx` files per component. NFR-4 requires specific a11y behaviors (keyboard operability, aria-labels, focus trap/restoration on `ProfileModal`, live-region `Toast`) that are best verified with interaction tests rather than manual QA.\n\n**Decision:** Extend the existing per-component `*.test.jsx`/`*.test.js` pattern for new behavior (filter/sort/pagination logic in `filterItems.js`, favorites toggle, modal focus management) using the already-installed test stack. No new test runner or a11y-testing library.\n\n**Consequences:** Keeps NFR-3's no-new-dependency constraint intact while giving the design phase a concrete place to specify testable behavior for NFR-4.\n\n---\n\n**Status:** complete\n","numLines":56,"startLine":1,"totalLines":56}}}
