{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CgT3y9rnqiFzN9ahsmr6n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015tjPChzntM7wK7yF5DUdBh","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/requirements-analysis-notes.md","content":"# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-05\n\nSupporting document for `arch-design-system`. Not a primary phase output.\nSource: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (31 requirements — 24 FR + 7\nNFR, 97% confidence, 2 MEDIUM-confidence blockers per\n`handoff-to-architect.json`). Scope is the Sections/Courses/Enrollment PRD:\nSection/Course/Enrollment data model, admin Sections and Courses CRUD UIs,\nthe roster auto-enroll/auto-drop engine, capacity enforcement, the public\ncourse surface (`/courses/[courseId]`, course tabs, `/my-courses`), course\naccess control, student-side course tab, navigation, and the REST API\nsurface.\n\nArchitectural concern buckets used below: **Data Model**, **Admin Sections\nUI**, **Section Detail & Membership**, **Core Courses Bootstrap**, **Roster\nEnrollment Engine**, **Year Rollover**, **Admin Courses UI**, **Course Public\nSurface**, **Auth/Access Control**, **My Courses Page**, **Navigation**,\n**Student Tab Integration**, **REST API Surface**, **Audit Logging**,\n**Design System (MUI-only)**, **Performance**, **Privacy/Roster Scrubbing**,\n**Test Harness**, **Scope Discipline**.\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| FR-1 | Data Model | `Section`, `SectionMember`, `Course`, `Enrollment` + `CourseKind`/`EnrollmentStatus` enums, with the specified unique constraints/index. Foundation tables — every other FR reads or writes one of these; must land before any service/route work. The `@@unique([studentId, academicYearId])` on `SectionMember` is also NFR-2's integrity backstop, not just a schema detail. |\n| FR-2 | Admin Sections UI | `/admin/sections` list: filter by academic year (defaults to current, reuses `AcademicYear`), table with capacity warning colour at >=90%. Read-only view over FR-1's tables plus a capacity/course-count aggregate query. |\n| FR-3 | Admin Sections UI | Section create/edit dialog incl. `UserPicker` (role=TEACHER) reuse and the \"one homeroom teacher per section per year\" rule. That uniqueness rule is app-level validation (not a DB constraint per FR-1) — `arch-design-system` should decide where it's enforced (service layer) since two concurrent writes could race. |\n| FR-4 | Section Detail & Membership | `/admin/sections/[id]`: members table (add/remove), course list, \"Create core courses\" button. Add/remove here is the trigger point for FR-7/FR-8's enrollment side effects — this page is a thin UI over the Roster Enrollment Engine, not its own business logic. |\n| FR-5 | Core Courses Bootstrap | \"Create core courses\" dialog: one CORE course per checked subject, enrolls all current section members. Must call the same course-creation path as FR-10 (creating a CORE course always enrolls current members) — one code path, two entry points (bulk vs. single). |\n| FR-6 | Year Rollover | \"Copy sections to next year\": grade level advanced by `order`, no members/teacher. Independent of the enrollment engine — a structural copy only, gated on a future non-current academic year existing. |\n| FR-7 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Auto-enroll on section add: ACTIVE-enrolls into every non-archived CORE course of the section; reactivates DROPPED rather than duplicating. Central business rule — same reactivate-vs-duplicate semantics FR-9/FR-10 must share. |\n| FR-8 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Auto-drop on section removal: CORE enrollments → DROPPED + `droppedAt`; electives untouched. Mirror of FR-7; both must be designed as one named module (e.g. `enrollInSectionCoreCourses` / `dropSectionCoreCourses`), not inlined per call site. |\n| FR-9 | Roster Enrollment Engine | `POST /api/students/:id/move-section` — FR-7 enroll + FR-8 drop in one DB transaction. The only place FR-7+FR-8 compose; confirms both must be transaction-safe, composable functions rather than route-level logic. |\n| FR-10 | Core Courses Bootstrap | New CORE course on a section enrolls all current members (ACTIVE). Same enroll path as FR-7 — triggered from course-creation instead of membership-add. |\n| FR-11 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Capacity enforcement: CONFLICT error with exact message pattern, \"Override capacity\" escape hatch. A guard that must sit in front of every enroll path (FR-7, FR-9, FR-10, and the course-level \"Add students\" of FR-15) — one shared capacity-check function, not four. |\n| FR-12 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Dropped enrollments keep historical data visible to admin/teacher but vanish from active rosters and add-student pickers. A query-filtering concern (`status = ACTIVE` everywhere user-facing lists are built) layered on top of FR-8's state transition. |\n| FR-13 | Admin Courses UI | `/admin/courses` list: filters (year/grade/subject/teacher/kind/search), reuses `Subject.color`. Read-only view, same shape as FR-2 but for courses. |\n| FR-14 | Admin Courses UI | Course create/edit dialog: subject, section (required iff kind=CORE), teacher + up to 3 co-teachers, auto-filled uppercase code. Validation rule (\"section required iff CORE\") needs to live in one shared validator the dialog and the API route both call. |\n| FR-15 | Admin Courses UI | `/admin/courses/[id]/enrollment`: roster table with drop/reactivate + add-students picker; CORE courses show an info note (membership managed via section). This page's \"Add students\" must still run through FR-11's capacity guard even though it bypasses the section-membership trigger. |\n| FR-16 | Course Public Surface | `/courses/[courseId]` header: subject colour band, teachers incl. co-teachers, section, room. Read-only composition of FR-1 data, gated by FR-18's `assertCanViewCourse`. |\n| FR-17 | Course Public Surface | New `src/config/course-tabs.ts` registry (mirrors `student-tabs.ts`, adds `roles`), registers Overview + Roster tabs with per-role visibility (student sees classmates' names only; parent tab hidden). Roster tab's student-facing truncation is also NFR-4's privacy rule — same tab, two render paths by role. |\n| FR-18 | Auth/Access Control | `src/server/auth/course-access.ts`: `assertCanViewCourse` / `assertCanManageCourse`. The authorization chokepoint every course route (FR-15, FR-16, FR-23) and the course-tabs registry (FR-17) must call — do not re-derive role logic per route. Built on top of NFR-7's existing `requireRole` stand-in, not a new auth primitive. |\n| FR-19 | My Courses Page | `/my-courses`: role-specific MUI Card grids (teacher/student/parent-by-child). Read view composing FR-18's access rules per role; parent grouping needs whatever parent-link read path already exists (brownfield reuse, same pattern as the prior PRD's family fan-out). |\n| FR-20 | Navigation | Sidebar entries: \"My courses\" (TEACHER/STUDENT/PARENT) + \"Sections\"/\"Courses\" under an \"Administration\" group for ADMIN. **MEDIUM confidence / flagged blocker**: `NavItem` has no `group` field today and no other admin pages exist yet. `arch-design-system` must decide: add a lightweight `group` field now, or ship flat entries until a dedicated admin-nav PRD lands — this is an explicit architect decision point, not a detail to infer silently. |\n| FR-21 | Student Tab Integration | Register `student-tabs.ts` \"Courses\" tab: student's courses, section, homeroom teacher. Pure read view, no new service — reuses FR-1 data + FR-18-equivalent visibility (whoever can view the student). |\n| FR-22 | Auth/Access Control | Extend `getAccessibleStudentIds(user)`: TEACHER additionally sees students via taught/co-taught course enrollments and their homeroom section. A brownfield extension to an existing exported function — must preserve its existing signature/contract for current PRD-01/PRD-04 callers, only add to the TEACHER branch. |\n| FR-23 | REST API Surface | Full endpoint list (sections CRUD+members+move-section+core-courses+copy-to-year; courses CRUD+roster+enrollments+status PATCH; `GET /api/me/courses`) — to be finalized verbatim by `arch-api-contracts`, following the existing envelope/pagination/search conventions. Every endpoint must map 1:1 to a service already named above (FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11 enrollment engine, FR-18 access control) — no net-new business logic at the route layer. |\n| FR-24 | Audit Logging | Every `section.*`/`course.*`/`enrollment.*` write records an `AuditLog` row via the existing `audit()` helper. A cross-cutting call that belongs at the service layer (alongside FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11's shared functions), not duplicated per route handler. |\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| NFR-1 | Design System (MUI-only) | Every visible element across sections/courses/enrollment screens is MUI/MUI-X only (DataTable, Autocomplete, Tabs, Dialog, Drawer, `useToast()`), looked up via the MUI MCP server first; no Tailwind/shadcn/Radix/react-select. Applies uniformly — unlike the prior PRD, there's no template-rendering exemption needed here (no React Email in scope). |\n| NFR-2 | Data Model | One-section-per-student-per-year enforced by the DB unique constraint on `SectionMember`, not just app code — directly backs FR-1's schema and is the safety net under FR-3's app-level homeroom-teacher-uniqueness check (which has no DB constraint and must be designed knowing that). |\n| NFR-3 | Performance | 30 students × 8 core courses (240 enrollments) in under 1s — a batching requirement on FR-7's auto-enroll path specifically; `arch-design-system` should spec `createMany`/batched writes the same way the enrollment engine is designed, not per-row inserts. |\n| NFR-4 | Privacy/Roster Scrubbing | Students see classmates' names only on the Roster tab and `GET /api/courses/:id/roster` — no email/admission number/profile link. A response-shape rule on FR-17's Roster tab and FR-23's roster endpoint; must be enforced server-side at the API layer (FR-23), not just hidden in the FR-17 UI. |\n| NFR-5 | Test Harness | Keep `lint`/`typecheck`/`test` green; add unit/integration/Playwright tests per Test Requirements. Scopes what `dev-planning` must schedule test-writing for; the roster engine (FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11) and capacity enforcement (FR-11) are the highest-value test targets given their reuse across routes. |\n| NFR-6 | Scope Discipline | No timetable slots, no course content, no prerequisites/course-request workflows, no automatic year-to-year promotion (only the manual FR-6 copy). Bounds `arch-design-system`'s design the same way it bounds dev's — don't let FR-6's \"copy\" grow into a promotion workflow. |\n| NFR-7 | Auth/Access Control | **Flagged blocker (MEDIUM confidence)**: `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` / `requireUser()` described in Platform Context do not exist yet — only `requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present (same gap PRD-04 flagged). New routes in this PRD (FR-23) must use the existing `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in rather than invent a new helper, until PRD-01 lands. This directly constrains FR-18's `course-access.ts` implementation — it should wrap `requireRole`, not assume `permissions.ts` exists. |\n\n## Cross-cutting notes for `arch-design-system`\n\n- **One enrollment engine, four callers**: FR-7 (add to section), FR-8\n  (remove from section), FR-9 (move-section transaction), and FR-10 (new\n  CORE course) all drive the same enroll/drop/reactivate semantics, and\n  FR-11's capacity guard plus FR-24's audit call must sit in front of/behind\n  all four. Design this as named, reusable service functions (e.g.\n  `enrollSectionCoreCourses`, `dropSectionCoreCourses`,\n  `checkCapacityOrThrow`), not duplicated per route — FR-9's single-transaction\n  requirement is the clearest signal these must already be composable,\n  transaction-safe units.\n- **FR-20's nav-grouping question is an explicit decision point**, not a\n  detail to quietly resolve — `handoff-to-architect.json` lists it as a\n  blocker. Record the chosen approach (add `group` to `NavItem` vs. flat\n  entries) in `system-design.md`'s Overview per Compliance Rule 5.\n- **NFR-7's auth-helper gap is also a carried-forward blocker** — FR-18's\n  `course-access.ts` must be designed against today's `requireRole.ts`\n  stand-in, not the not-yet-built `permissions.ts`/`requireUser()`. Surface\n  this explicitly in `system-design.md`'s Overview so `dev-planning` doesn't\n  schedule work against auth helpers that don't exist.\n- **Two independent app-level uniqueness rules have no DB backstop and need\n  an explicit concurrency decision**: FR-3's \"one homeroom teacher per\n  section per year\" and FR-14's \"section required iff kind=CORE\" are both\n  service-layer validations only (unlike NFR-2's DB-constrained rule) —\n  `arch-design-system` should say where each is checked and whether a race\n  (two concurrent section edits) is acceptable risk or needs a DB-level\n  constraint too.\n- **NFR-4's roster scrubbing must be enforced at the API layer (FR-23),\n  not just the FR-17 UI tab** — call this out explicitly in the\n  traceability table so the response-shape rule isn't treated as\n  presentation-only.\n- **FR-5 and FR-10 share one course-creation-enrolls-members code path** —\n  bulk (checked subjects) vs. single (one course) are two entry points into\n  the same underlying function, same pattern as the enrollment engine note\n  above.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10990,"cache_read_input_tokens":27713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10990},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8fc3ceb1-8385-4b37-8370-6806b83adbfd","uuid":"22c7a617-d7e9-4441-bb46-23aab538a220","timestamp":"2026-10-27T16:25:18.834Z","request_id":"req_011CgT3y9agAXTR9RZjdMrFR"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tjPChzntM7wK7yF5DUdBh","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/requirements-analysis-notes.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8fc3ceb1-8385-4b37-8370-6806b83adbfd","uuid":"360fd445-2dc7-409f-a2fe-c08d3d138975","timestamp":"2026-10-27T16:25:19.498Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub-prds/artifacts/architect/requirements-analysis-notes.md","content":"# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-05\n\nSupporting document for `arch-design-system`. Not a primary phase output.\nSource: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (31 requirements — 24 FR + 7\nNFR, 97% confidence, 2 MEDIUM-confidence blockers per\n`handoff-to-architect.json`). Scope is the Sections/Courses/Enrollment PRD:\nSection/Course/Enrollment data model, admin Sections and Courses CRUD UIs,\nthe roster auto-enroll/auto-drop engine, capacity enforcement, the public\ncourse surface (`/courses/[courseId]`, course tabs, `/my-courses`), course\naccess control, student-side course tab, navigation, and the REST API\nsurface.\n\nArchitectural concern buckets used below: **Data Model**, **Admin Sections\nUI**, **Section Detail & Membership**, **Core Courses Bootstrap**, **Roster\nEnrollment Engine**, **Year Rollover**, **Admin Courses UI**, **Course Public\nSurface**, **Auth/Access Control**, **My Courses Page**, **Navigation**,\n**Student Tab Integration**, **REST API Surface**, **Audit Logging**,\n**Design System (MUI-only)**, **Performance**, **Privacy/Roster Scrubbing**,\n**Test Harness**, **Scope Discipline**.\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| FR-1 | Data Model | `Section`, `SectionMember`, `Course`, `Enrollment` + `CourseKind`/`EnrollmentStatus` enums, with the specified unique constraints/index. Foundation tables — every other FR reads or writes one of these; must land before any service/route work. The `@@unique([studentId, academicYearId])` on `SectionMember` is also NFR-2's integrity backstop, not just a schema detail. |\n| FR-2 | Admin Sections UI | `/admin/sections` list: filter by academic year (defaults to current, reuses `AcademicYear`), table with capacity warning colour at >=90%. Read-only view over FR-1's tables plus a capacity/course-count aggregate query. |\n| FR-3 | Admin Sections UI | Section create/edit dialog incl. `UserPicker` (role=TEACHER) reuse and the \"one homeroom teacher per section per year\" rule. That uniqueness rule is app-level validation (not a DB constraint per FR-1) — `arch-design-system` should decide where it's enforced (service layer) since two concurrent writes could race. |\n| FR-4 | Section Detail & Membership | `/admin/sections/[id]`: members table (add/remove), course list, \"Create core courses\" button. Add/remove here is the trigger point for FR-7/FR-8's enrollment side effects — this page is a thin UI over the Roster Enrollment Engine, not its own business logic. |\n| FR-5 | Core Courses Bootstrap | \"Create core courses\" dialog: one CORE course per checked subject, enrolls all current section members. Must call the same course-creation path as FR-10 (creating a CORE course always enrolls current members) — one code path, two entry points (bulk vs. single). |\n| FR-6 | Year Rollover | \"Copy sections to next year\": grade level advanced by `order`, no members/teacher. Independent of the enrollment engine — a structural copy only, gated on a future non-current academic year existing. |\n| FR-7 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Auto-enroll on section add: ACTIVE-enrolls into every non-archived CORE course of the section; reactivates DROPPED rather than duplicating. Central business rule — same reactivate-vs-duplicate semantics FR-9/FR-10 must share. |\n| FR-8 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Auto-drop on section removal: CORE enrollments → DROPPED + `droppedAt`; electives untouched. Mirror of FR-7; both must be designed as one named module (e.g. `enrollInSectionCoreCourses` / `dropSectionCoreCourses`), not inlined per call site. |\n| FR-9 | Roster Enrollment Engine | `POST /api/students/:id/move-section` — FR-7 enroll + FR-8 drop in one DB transaction. The only place FR-7+FR-8 compose; confirms both must be transaction-safe, composable functions rather than route-level logic. |\n| FR-10 | Core Courses Bootstrap | New CORE course on a section enrolls all current members (ACTIVE). Same enroll path as FR-7 — triggered from course-creation instead of membership-add. |\n| FR-11 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Capacity enforcement: CONFLICT error with exact message pattern, \"Override capacity\" escape hatch. A guard that must sit in front of every enroll path (FR-7, FR-9, FR-10, and the course-level \"Add students\" of FR-15) — one shared capacity-check function, not four. |\n| FR-12 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Dropped enrollments keep historical data visible to admin/teacher but vanish from active rosters and add-student pickers. A query-filtering concern (`status = ACTIVE` everywhere user-facing lists are built) layered on top of FR-8's state transition. |\n| FR-13 | Admin Courses UI | `/admin/courses` list: filters (year/grade/subject/teacher/kind/search), reuses `Subject.color`. Read-only view, same shape as FR-2 but for courses. |\n| FR-14 | Admin Courses UI | Course create/edit dialog: subject, section (required iff kind=CORE), teacher + up to 3 co-teachers, auto-filled uppercase code. Validation rule (\"section required iff CORE\") needs to live in one shared validator the dialog and the API route both call. |\n| FR-15 | Admin Courses UI | `/admin/courses/[id]/enrollment`: roster table with drop/reactivate + add-students picker; CORE courses show an info note (membership managed via section). This page's \"Add students\" must still run through FR-11's capacity guard even though it bypasses the section-membership trigger. |\n| FR-16 | Course Public Surface | `/courses/[courseId]` header: subject colour band, teachers incl. co-teachers, section, room. Read-only composition of FR-1 data, gated by FR-18's `assertCanViewCourse`. |\n| FR-17 | Course Public Surface | New `src/config/course-tabs.ts` registry (mirrors `student-tabs.ts`, adds `roles`), registers Overview + Roster tabs with per-role visibility (student sees classmates' names only; parent tab hidden). Roster tab's student-facing truncation is also NFR-4's privacy rule — same tab, two render paths by role. |\n| FR-18 | Auth/Access Control | `src/server/auth/course-access.ts`: `assertCanViewCourse` / `assertCanManageCourse`. The authorization chokepoint every course route (FR-15, FR-16, FR-23) and the course-tabs registry (FR-17) must call — do not re-derive role logic per route. Built on top of NFR-7's existing `requireRole` stand-in, not a new auth primitive. |\n| FR-19 | My Courses Page | `/my-courses`: role-specific MUI Card grids (teacher/student/parent-by-child). Read view composing FR-18's access rules per role; parent grouping needs whatever parent-link read path already exists (brownfield reuse, same pattern as the prior PRD's family fan-out). |\n| FR-20 | Navigation | Sidebar entries: \"My courses\" (TEACHER/STUDENT/PARENT) + \"Sections\"/\"Courses\" under an \"Administration\" group for ADMIN. **MEDIUM confidence / flagged blocker**: `NavItem` has no `group` field today and no other admin pages exist yet. `arch-design-system` must decide: add a lightweight `group` field now, or ship flat entries until a dedicated admin-nav PRD lands — this is an explicit architect decision point, not a detail to infer silently. |\n| FR-21 | Student Tab Integration | Register `student-tabs.ts` \"Courses\" tab: student's courses, section, homeroom teacher. Pure read view, no new service — reuses FR-1 data + FR-18-equivalent visibility (whoever can view the student). |\n| FR-22 | Auth/Access Control | Extend `getAccessibleStudentIds(user)`: TEACHER additionally sees students via taught/co-taught course enrollments and their homeroom section. A brownfield extension to an existing exported function — must preserve its existing signature/contract for current PRD-01/PRD-04 callers, only add to the TEACHER branch. |\n| FR-23 | REST API Surface | Full endpoint list (sections CRUD+members+move-section+core-courses+copy-to-year; courses CRUD+roster+enrollments+status PATCH; `GET /api/me/courses`) — to be finalized verbatim by `arch-api-contracts`, following the existing envelope/pagination/search conventions. Every endpoint must map 1:1 to a service already named above (FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11 enrollment engine, FR-18 access control) — no net-new business logic at the route layer. |\n| FR-24 | Audit Logging | Every `section.*`/`course.*`/`enrollment.*` write records an `AuditLog` row via the existing `audit()` helper. A cross-cutting call that belongs at the service layer (alongside FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11's shared functions), not duplicated per route handler. |\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| NFR-1 | Design System (MUI-only) | Every visible element across sections/courses/enrollment screens is MUI/MUI-X only (DataTable, Autocomplete, Tabs, Dialog, Drawer, `useToast()`), looked up via the MUI MCP server first; no Tailwind/shadcn/Radix/react-select. Applies uniformly — unlike the prior PRD, there's no template-rendering exemption needed here (no React Email in scope). |\n| NFR-2 | Data Model | One-section-per-student-per-year enforced by the DB unique constraint on `SectionMember`, not just app code — directly backs FR-1's schema and is the safety net under FR-3's app-level homeroom-teacher-uniqueness check (which has no DB constraint and must be designed knowing that). |\n| NFR-3 | Performance | 30 students × 8 core courses (240 enrollments) in under 1s — a batching requirement on FR-7's auto-enroll path specifically; `arch-design-system` should spec `createMany`/batched writes the same way the enrollment engine is designed, not per-row inserts. |\n| NFR-4 | Privacy/Roster Scrubbing | Students see classmates' names only on the Roster tab and `GET /api/courses/:id/roster` — no email/admission number/profile link. A response-shape rule on FR-17's Roster tab and FR-23's roster endpoint; must be enforced server-side at the API layer (FR-23), not just hidden in the FR-17 UI. |\n| NFR-5 | Test Harness | Keep `lint`/`typecheck`/`test` green; add unit/integration/Playwright tests per Test Requirements. Scopes what `dev-planning` must schedule test-writing for; the roster engine (FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11) and capacity enforcement (FR-11) are the highest-value test targets given their reuse across routes. |\n| NFR-6 | Scope Discipline | No timetable slots, no course content, no prerequisites/course-request workflows, no automatic year-to-year promotion (only the manual FR-6 copy). Bounds `arch-design-system`'s design the same way it bounds dev's — don't let FR-6's \"copy\" grow into a promotion workflow. |\n| NFR-7 | Auth/Access Control | **Flagged blocker (MEDIUM confidence)**: `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` / `requireUser()` described in Platform Context do not exist yet — only `requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present (same gap PRD-04 flagged). New routes in this PRD (FR-23) must use the existing `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in rather than invent a new helper, until PRD-01 lands. This directly constrains FR-18's `course-access.ts` implementation — it should wrap `requireRole`, not assume `permissions.ts` exists. |\n\n## Cross-cutting notes for `arch-design-system`\n\n- **One enrollment engine, four callers**: FR-7 (add to section), FR-8\n  (remove from section), FR-9 (move-section transaction), and FR-10 (new\n  CORE course) all drive the same enroll/drop/reactivate semantics, and\n  FR-11's capacity guard plus FR-24's audit call must sit in front of/behind\n  all four. Design this as named, reusable service functions (e.g.\n  `enrollSectionCoreCourses`, `dropSectionCoreCourses`,\n  `checkCapacityOrThrow`), not duplicated per route — FR-9's single-transaction\n  requirement is the clearest signal these must already be composable,\n  transaction-safe units.\n- **FR-20's nav-grouping question is an explicit decision point**, not a\n  detail to quietly resolve — `handoff-to-architect.json` lists it as a\n  blocker. Record the chosen approach (add `group` to `NavItem` vs. flat\n  entries) in `system-design.md`'s Overview per Compliance Rule 5.\n- **NFR-7's auth-helper gap is also a carried-forward blocker** — FR-18's\n  `course-access.ts` must be designed against today's `requireRole.ts`\n  stand-in, not the not-yet-built `permissions.ts`/`requireUser()`. Surface\n  this explicitly in `system-design.md`'s Overview so `dev-planning` doesn't\n  schedule work against auth helpers that don't exist.\n- **Two independent app-level uniqueness rules have no DB backstop and need\n  an explicit concurrency decision**: FR-3's \"one homeroom teacher per\n  section per year\" and FR-14's \"section required iff kind=CORE\" are both\n  service-layer validations only (unlike NFR-2's DB-constrained rule) —\n  `arch-design-system` should say where each is checked and whether a race\n  (two concurrent section edits) is acceptable risk or needs a DB-level\n  constraint too.\n- **NFR-4's roster scrubbing must be enforced at the API layer (FR-23),\n  not just the FR-17 UI tab** — call this out explicitly in the\n  traceability table so the response-shape rule isn't treated as\n  presentation-only.\n- **FR-5 and FR-10 share one course-creation-enrolls-members code path** —\n  bulk (checked subjects) vs. single (one course) are two entry points into\n  the same underlying function, same pattern as the enrollment engine note\n  above.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":96,"newStart":1,"newLines":96,"lines":["-# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-04","+# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-05"," "," Supporting document for `arch-design-system`. Not a primary phase output.","-Source: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (34 requirements — 24 FR + 10","-NFR, 97% confidence, no blockers per `handoff-to-architect.json`). Scope is","-the Notifications & Email PRD: notification data model/registry, `notify()`","-service, email templates, an email outbox + dispatcher worker, bell/popover","-UI, the `/notifications` page, preferences UI, and the admin email log.","+Source: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (31 requirements — 24 FR + 7","+NFR, 97% confidence, 2 MEDIUM-confidence blockers per","+`handoff-to-architect.json`). Scope is the Sections/Courses/Enrollment PRD:","+Section/Course/Enrollment data model, admin Sections and Courses CRUD UIs,","+the roster auto-enroll/auto-drop engine, capacity enforcement, the public","+course surface (`/courses/[courseId]`, course tabs, `/my-courses`), course","+access control, student-side course tab, navigation, and the REST API","+surface."," ","-Architectural concern buckets used below: **Data Model**, **Notification","-Type Registry**, **Notification Service**, **Family Fan-out Helper**, **Email","-Templates**, **Auth Email Integration**, **Email Dispatcher**, **Email","-Transport Config**, **Bell/Popover UI**, **Notifications Page**, **Retention","-Job**, **Preferences UI**, **Preferences API**, **Admin Email Log**, **REST","-API Surface**, **Navigation**, **Design System (MUI-only)**, **Security/","-Access Control**, **Test Harness**, **Scope Discipline**.","+Architectural concern buckets used below: **Data Model**, **Admin Sections","+UI**, **Section Detail & Membership**, **Core Courses Bootstrap**, **Roster","+Enrollment Engine**, **Year Rollover**, **Admin Courses UI**, **Course Public","+Surface**, **Auth/Access Control**, **My Courses Page**, **Navigation**,","+**Student Tab Integration**, **REST API Surface**, **Audit Logging**,","+**Design System (MUI-only)**, **Performance**, **Privacy/Roster Scrubbing**,","+**Test Harness**, **Scope Discipline**."," "," ## Functional Requirements"," "," | ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |"," |----|------------------------|-------|","-| FR-1 | Data Model | `Notification`, `NotificationPreference`, `EmailOutbox` + `EmailStatus` enum. Foundation tables — every other FR reads or writes one of these; must land before any service/route work. |","-| FR-2 | Notification Type Registry | Typed registry at `src/server/notifications/types.ts` (label/description/category/defaultEmail/mandatoryInApp/roles), seeded with `account.invite`, `account.password_reset`, `system.test`. Single source of truth the service layer (FR-4), preferences UI (FR-18), and compile-time typing (FR-3) all key off. |","-| FR-3 | Notification Service | `notify()` must reject unknown type keys at compile time (TS literal/union against the registry) — a typing constraint on FR-4's signature, not a runtime check. |","-| FR-4 | Notification Service | Core `notify({ type, userIds, title, body?, link?, email? })` — skips archived/disabled users, resolves preferences (falling back to registry defaults), writes `Notification` rows and `EmailOutbox` rows as applicable. The central chokepoint everything else (FR-6, FR-9, FR-20, FR-24) calls into. |","-| FR-5 | Notification Service | Batching via `createMany` (≤3 round-trips per 1,000 users each for notifications/emails) + never-throw-to-caller (log failures) + \"call after your own transaction commits\" contract. Directly drives NFR-2's latency budget and the service's error-handling shape. |","-| FR-6 | Family Fan-out Helper | `notifyStudentAndParents({ studentIds, ...rest })` — expands student → student + linked parents, deduped, for later PRDs to reuse. Thin wrapper over FR-4 plus whatever parent-link read path already exists (brownfield reuse, not a new link model). |","-| FR-7 | Email Templates | `@react-email/components` templates under `src/server/email/templates/`, rendered to HTML + plain text, sharing a base layout (header/content/footer, preferences link, disclosure sentence). One shared layout component — avoid three independently-styled templates. |","-| FR-8 | Email Templates | Concrete templates: invite, password-reset, generic-notification, each with their specified fields/CTA. Three thin wrappers over FR-7's base layout. |","-| FR-9 | Auth Email Integration | Replace the PRD-01 `sendInvite()` stub (`src/server/services/users.ts:385-390`, currently log-only) with `sendAuthEmail()` that enqueues via `EmailOutbox`. A brownfield swap at an existing call site — must not change the stub's call signature/contract that PRD-01 callers depend on. |","-| FR-10 | Email Dispatcher | `dispatchEmails({ batchSize = 50 })` — `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` select of due PENDING rows, mark SENDING, send via Nodemailer, mark SENT or schedule retry. The other central chokepoint; must be designed as one module both the HTTP route (FR-12) and the worker loop (FR-12) call. |","-| FR-11 | Email Dispatcher | Retry backoff schedule (1/5/15/60/240 min across 5 attempts, FAILED after 5th) — state-machine detail inside FR-10's dispatch logic, also what FR-20's \"Retry now\" action resets. |","-| FR-12 | Email Dispatcher | Two callers of `dispatchEmails`: `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch` (bearer `CRON_SECRET`, ties to NFR-8) and `npm run worker:email` (15s loop, local/simple deployments). Same underlying function, two thin entry points — do not fork the dispatch logic between them. |","-| FR-13 | Email Transport Config | Env vars (`SMTP_*`, `EMAIL_FROM`, `CRON_SECRET`, `EMAIL_TRANSPORT` smtp\\|console). Console transport is a dev-mode Nodemailer transport swap, not a separate code path through the dispatcher. |","-| FR-14 | Bell/Popover UI | Live unread-count bell (MUI IconButton + Badge) replacing the static badge at `src/app/(app)/shell.tsx:141`; polls `GET /api/notifications/unread-count` every 30s, paused on `visibilitychange` hidden, \"9+\" cap. Read-only UI over FR-21's unread-count endpoint; polling pause/resume is a client-side concern only. |","-| FR-15 | Bell/Popover UI | Popover with 10 most recent notifications (unread styling, 2-line clamp, relative time), click marks-read + navigates; footer \"Mark all as read\" / \"View all\". Composes FR-21's list/read endpoints; no new server logic beyond what FR-16/FR-21 already define. |","-| FR-16 | Notifications Page | `/notifications`: paginated (20/page), All/Unread tabs, category filter, bulk mark-all-read, per-row read/unread toggle. Same list/read API surface as FR-15's popover — one list service backing both, not two. |","-| FR-17 | Retention Job | `purgeOldNotifications()` — delete notifications older than 180 days, invoked once/day from the dispatcher endpoint with in-memory last-run tracking (re-run after restart is acceptable). Piggybacks on FR-12's dispatch endpoint call site — not a separate cron/queue. |","-| FR-18 | Preferences UI | `/account/notifications`: MUI Table grouped by category, In-app/Email switches per visible type, mandatory in-app types shown disabled with tooltip. Pure read+toggle view over FR-2 (registry) + FR-1 (`NotificationPreference` rows). |","-| FR-19 | Preferences API | `PUT /api/me/notification-preferences` saves immediately, optimistic UI + rollback on error — client-side concern (FR-18) paired with the server validation in FR-22. |","-| FR-20 | Admin Email Log | `/admin/email-log`: table (created/recipient/subject/status/attempts/last-error), status filter, \"Retry now\" (resets attempts=0/status=PENDING/nextAttemptAt=now), \"Send test notification to me\" (calls `notify({ type: 'system.test', ... })`). Read/write surface directly over `EmailOutbox` (FR-1) and `notify()` (FR-4); \"Retry now\" must use the same reset semantics FR-11's backoff expects. |","-| FR-21 | REST API Surface | Full endpoint list (notifications CRUD-ish, preferences, dispatch, admin email-log, admin test) — to be finalized verbatim by `arch-api-contracts`, following PRD-00's envelope/pagination/error-code conventions. Every endpoint here must map 1:1 to a service already named in FR-4/FR-10/FR-16/FR-18/FR-20; no net-new business logic at the route layer. |","-| FR-22 | Preferences API | `PUT /api/me/notification-preferences` must reject `inApp=false` for `mandatoryInApp` types — server-side enforcement mirroring FR-18's disabled-switch UI (defense in depth, UI alone is not sufficient). |","-| FR-23 | Navigation | Sidebar nav entries for notifications page (+ admin email-log) in `src/config/navigation.ts`, following the existing `NavItem` convention (currently only Dashboard). MEDIUM confidence on exact entries — flag for `arch-design-system` to confirm naming/grouping. |","-| FR-24 | Data Model (seed) | Seed 3 `system.test` `Notification` rows (1 read, 2 unread) for two existing seeded users. Pure seed-data task, depends on FR-1's schema and FR-2's registry key existing first. |","+| FR-1 | Data Model | `Section`, `SectionMember`, `Course`, `Enrollment` + `CourseKind`/`EnrollmentStatus` enums, with the specified unique constraints/index. Foundation tables — every other FR reads or writes one of these; must land before any service/route work. The `@@unique([studentId, academicYearId])` on `SectionMember` is also NFR-2's integrity backstop, not just a schema detail. |","+| FR-2 | Admin Sections UI | `/admin/sections` list: filter by academic year (defaults to current, reuses `AcademicYear`), table with capacity warning colour at >=90%. Read-only view over FR-1's tables plus a capacity/course-count aggregate query. |","+| FR-3 | Admin Sections UI | Section create/edit dialog incl. `UserPicker` (role=TEACHER) reuse and the \"one homeroom teacher per section per year\" rule. That uniqueness rule is app-level validation (not a DB constraint per FR-1) — `arch-design-system` should decide where it's enforced (service layer) since two concurrent writes could race. |","+| FR-4 | Section Detail & Membership | `/admin/sections/[id]`: members table (add/remove), course list, \"Create core courses\" button. Add/remove here is the trigger point for FR-7/FR-8's enrollment side effects — this page is a thin UI over the Roster Enrollment Engine, not its own business logic. |","+| FR-5 | Core Courses Bootstrap | \"Create core courses\" dialog: one CORE course per checked subject, enrolls all current section members. Must call the same course-creation path as FR-10 (creating a CORE course always enrolls current members) — one code path, two entry points (bulk vs. single). |","+| FR-6 | Year Rollover | \"Copy sections to next year\": grade level advanced by `order`, no members/teacher. Independent of the enrollment engine — a structural copy only, gated on a future non-current academic year existing. |","+| FR-7 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Auto-enroll on section add: ACTIVE-enrolls into every non-archived CORE course of the section; reactivates DROPPED rather than duplicating. Central business rule — same reactivate-vs-duplicate semantics FR-9/FR-10 must share. |","+| FR-8 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Auto-drop on section removal: CORE enrollments → DROPPED + `droppedAt`; electives untouched. Mirror of FR-7; both must be designed as one named module (e.g. `enrollInSectionCoreCourses` / `dropSectionCoreCourses`), not inlined per call site. |","+| FR-9 | Roster Enrollment Engine | `POST /api/students/:id/move-section` — FR-7 enroll + FR-8 drop in one DB transaction. The only place FR-7+FR-8 compose; confirms both must be transaction-safe, composable functions rather than route-level logic. |","+| FR-10 | Core Courses Bootstrap | New CORE course on a section enrolls all current members (ACTIVE). Same enroll path as FR-7 — triggered from course-creation instead of membership-add. |","+| FR-11 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Capacity enforcement: CONFLICT error with exact message pattern, \"Override capacity\" escape hatch. A guard that must sit in front of every enroll path (FR-7, FR-9, FR-10, and the course-level \"Add students\" of FR-15) — one shared capacity-check function, not four. |","+| FR-12 | Roster Enrollment Engine | Dropped enrollments keep historical data visible to admin/teacher but vanish from active rosters and add-student pickers. A query-filtering concern (`status = ACTIVE` everywhere user-facing lists are built) layered on top of FR-8's state transition. |","+| FR-13 | Admin Courses UI | `/admin/courses` list: filters (year/grade/subject/teacher/kind/search), reuses `Subject.color`. Read-only view, same shape as FR-2 but for courses. |","+| FR-14 | Admin Courses UI | Course create/edit dialog: subject, section (required iff kind=CORE), teacher + up to 3 co-teachers, auto-filled uppercase code. Validation rule (\"section required iff CORE\") needs to live in one shared validator the dialog and the API route both call. |","+| FR-15 | Admin Courses UI | `/admin/courses/[id]/enrollment`: roster table with drop/reactivate + add-students picker; CORE courses show an info note (membership managed via section). This page's \"Add students\" must still run through FR-11's capacity guard even though it bypasses the section-membership trigger. |","+| FR-16 | Course Public Surface | `/courses/[courseId]` header: subject colour band, teachers incl. co-teachers, section, room. Read-only composition of FR-1 data, gated by FR-18's `assertCanViewCourse`. |","+| FR-17 | Course Public Surface | New `src/config/course-tabs.ts` registry (mirrors `student-tabs.ts`, adds `roles`), registers Overview + Roster tabs with per-role visibility (student sees classmates' names only; parent tab hidden). Roster tab's student-facing truncation is also NFR-4's privacy rule — same tab, two render paths by role. |","+| FR-18 | Auth/Access Control | `src/server/auth/course-access.ts`: `assertCanViewCourse` / `assertCanManageCourse`. The authorization chokepoint every course route (FR-15, FR-16, FR-23) and the course-tabs registry (FR-17) must call — do not re-derive role logic per route. Built on top of NFR-7's existing `requireRole` stand-in, not a new auth primitive. |","+| FR-19 | My Courses Page | `/my-courses`: role-specific MUI Card grids (teacher/student/parent-by-child). Read view composing FR-18's access rules per role; parent grouping needs whatever parent-link read path already exists (brownfield reuse, same pattern as the prior PRD's family fan-out). |","+| FR-20 | Navigation | Sidebar entries: \"My courses\" (TEACHER/STUDENT/PARENT) + \"Sections\"/\"Courses\" under an \"Administration\" group for ADMIN. **MEDIUM confidence / flagged blocker**: `NavItem` has no `group` field today and no other admin pages exist yet. `arch-design-system` must decide: add a lightweight `group` field now, or ship flat entries until a dedicated admin-nav PRD lands — this is an explicit architect decision point, not a detail to infer silently. |","+| FR-21 | Student Tab Integration | Register `student-tabs.ts` \"Courses\" tab: student's courses, section, homeroom teacher. Pure read view, no new service — reuses FR-1 data + FR-18-equivalent visibility (whoever can view the student). |","+| FR-22 | Auth/Access Control | Extend `getAccessibleStudentIds(user)`: TEACHER additionally sees students via taught/co-taught course enrollments and their homeroom section. A brownfield extension to an existing exported function — must preserve its existing signature/contract for current PRD-01/PRD-04 callers, only add to the TEACHER branch. |","+| FR-23 | REST API Surface | Full endpoint list (sections CRUD+members+move-section+core-courses+copy-to-year; courses CRUD+roster+enrollments+status PATCH; `GET /api/me/courses`) — to be finalized verbatim by `arch-api-contracts`, following the existing envelope/pagination/search conventions. Every endpoint must map 1:1 to a service already named above (FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11 enrollment engine, FR-18 access control) — no net-new business logic at the route layer. |","+| FR-24 | Audit Logging | Every `section.*`/`course.*`/`enrollment.*` write records an `AuditLog` row via the existing `audit()` helper. A cross-cutting call that belongs at the service layer (alongside FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11's shared functions), not duplicated per route handler. |"," "," ## Non-Functional Requirements"," "," | ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |"," |----|------------------------|-------|","-| NFR-1 | Design System (MUI-only) | Every visible surface (bell, popover, notifications page, preferences table, admin log) MUI-only, checked via MUI MCP, no banned libraries; email templates explicitly exempt (use `@react-email/components` instead). A lint-enforced boundary (`no-restricted-imports`) `arch-design-system` should scope per-directory so the email-template exemption doesn't leak into app UI code. |","-| NFR-2 | Notification Service | `notify()` for 1,000 recipients < 2s — directly validates FR-5's ≤3-round-trip batching design; a perf budget the service implementation must be benchmarked against. |","-| NFR-3 | Notifications Page / Bell | `GET /api/notifications/unread-count` < 50ms, backed by a `(userId, readAt, createdAt)` index — constrains the `Notification` table's indexing (FR-1) and the query shape of FR-14/FR-21's unread-count endpoint. |","-| NFR-4 | Email Dispatcher | No double-send under concurrent dispatchers (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`) + stuck-`SENDING`-for->10min recovery back to PENDING. A concurrency-safety invariant on FR-10's dispatch query — the stuck-row recovery is an extra step FR-10/FR-12 must schedule (e.g. at the top of each dispatch call), not an afterthought. |","-| NFR-5 | Security/Access Control | Notification `link` must be a validated relative path (`/`-prefixed, open-redirect prevention); email HTML must escape all user-provided text. Two related but distinct sanitization rules — one on `notify()`'s input (FR-4), one on the email template renderer (FR-7). |","-| NFR-6 | Email Templates | Every outbound email has HTML + plain-text parts + a `List-Unsubscribe` header pointing at the preferences page. Extends FR-7's base layout contract and FR-10's send step (header must be set at send time, not template-render time). |","-| NFR-7 | Security/Access Control | Server-side `requireUser()`/`requireRole()` on every route: own-notifications-only for user routes, ADMIN-only for email-log routes, `CRON_SECRET` bearer for the internal dispatch route. A per-route authorization matrix `arch-api-contracts` must encode explicitly against FR-21's endpoint list — hiding UI (FR-20's admin page) is never sufficient. |","-| NFR-8 | Email Dispatcher | Dispatch without correct `CRON_SECRET` bearer → 401 via the standard error envelope. The negative-path companion to FR-12/NFR-7's auth check on that one route. |","-| NFR-9 | Test Harness | Keep `lint`/`typecheck`/`test` green; add the unit/integration/Playwright tests listed in Test Requirements. Scopes exactly what `dev-planning` must schedule test-writing for. |","-| NFR-10 | Scope Discipline | No SMS/push, no websockets (bell polls only), no digest emails — bounds `arch-design-system`'s design the same way it bounds dev's; don't let FR-14's polling design grow into a push/websocket architecture. |","+| NFR-1 | Design System (MUI-only) | Every visible element across sections/courses/enrollment screens is MUI/MUI-X only (DataTable, Autocomplete, Tabs, Dialog, Drawer, `useToast()`), looked up via the MUI MCP server first; no Tailwind/shadcn/Radix/react-select. Applies uniformly — unlike the prior PRD, there's no template-rendering exemption needed here (no React Email in scope). |","+| NFR-2 | Data Model | One-section-per-student-per-year enforced by the DB unique constraint on `SectionMember`, not just app code — directly backs FR-1's schema and is the safety net under FR-3's app-level homeroom-teacher-uniqueness check (which has no DB constraint and must be designed knowing that). |","+| NFR-3 | Performance | 30 students × 8 core courses (240 enrollments) in under 1s — a batching requirement on FR-7's auto-enroll path specifically; `arch-design-system` should spec `createMany`/batched writes the same way the enrollment engine is designed, not per-row inserts. |","+| NFR-4 | Privacy/Roster Scrubbing | Students see classmates' names only on the Roster tab and `GET /api/courses/:id/roster` — no email/admission number/profile link. A response-shape rule on FR-17's Roster tab and FR-23's roster endpoint; must be enforced server-side at the API layer (FR-23), not just hidden in the FR-17 UI. |","+| NFR-5 | Test Harness | Keep `lint`/`typecheck`/`test` green; add unit/integration/Playwright tests per Test Requirements. Scopes what `dev-planning` must schedule test-writing for; the roster engine (FR-7/FR-8/FR-9/FR-11) and capacity enforcement (FR-11) are the highest-value test targets given their reuse across routes. |","+| NFR-6 | Scope Discipline | No timetable slots, no course content, no prerequisites/course-request workflows, no automatic year-to-year promotion (only the manual FR-6 copy). Bounds `arch-design-system`'s design the same way it bounds dev's — don't let FR-6's \"copy\" grow into a promotion workflow. |","+| NFR-7 | Auth/Access Control | **Flagged blocker (MEDIUM confidence)**: `src/server/auth/permissions.ts` / `requireUser()` described in Platform Context do not exist yet — only `requireRole.ts` and `student-access.ts` are present (same gap PRD-04 flagged). New routes in this PRD (FR-23) must use the existing `requireRole(...ALL_ROLES)` stand-in rather than invent a new helper, until PRD-01 lands. This directly constrains FR-18's `course-access.ts` implementation — it should wrap `requireRole`, not assume `permissions.ts` exists. |"," "," ## Cross-cutting notes for `arch-design-system`"," ","-- **Two chokepoints to design as named, reusable modules, not inline","-  code**: `notify()` (FR-3/FR-4/FR-5/FR-6, in","-  `src/server/notifications/notify.ts`) and `dispatchEmails()` (FR-10/FR-11,","-  called identically by FR-12's HTTP route and worker loop). Nearly every","-  other FR is either a thin UI/read-view wrapper around these two, or a","-  template/config detail feeding them (FR-2 registry, FR-7/FR-8 templates,","-  FR-13 env config).","-- **One list/read service backs both the popover (FR-15) and the","-  `/notifications` page (FR-16)** — same pagination/filter/mark-read","-  surface, just different page sizes/UI chrome. Don't let `arch-design-system`","-  spec two separate query paths.","-- **Defense-in-depth pairs to call out explicitly in the traceability","-  table**: FR-18 (disabled switch) + FR-22 (server-side reject) for","-  mandatory in-app types; FR-9's brownfield stub swap must preserve","-  whatever call signature PRD-01's `sendInvite()` callers already use","-  (NFR-7-style reuse discipline, even though this PRD's NFRs don't","-  re-state it — see FR-9's own source note referencing","-  `src/server/services/users.ts:385-390`).","-- **NFR-4's stuck-`SENDING` recovery and FR-17's daily purge both need a","-  concrete trigger point** — likely \"run at the top of `dispatchEmails()`","-  calls, gated by in-memory last-run timestamps,\" per FR-17's own","-  acceptance of restart-driven re-runs. `arch-design-system` should pick","-  one scheduling mechanism for both rather than inventing two.","-- **FR-23's exact nav entries are MEDIUM confidence** (inferred from","-  layout conventions, not spelled out) — flag in `system-design.md`'s","-  traceability table so `dev-planning` knows the nav copy/grouping needs a","-  final decision, not just an endpoint contract.","-- **NFR-1's MUI-only rule needs a directory boundary, not a blanket rule**:","-  `src/server/email/templates/**` is explicitly exempt (React Email","-  components) while every app-UI path (bell, popover, `/notifications`,","-  `/account/notifications`, `/admin/email-log`) is MUI-only. Worth a single","-  sentence in `system-design.md`'s Overview so the ESLint config `dev`","-  writes doesn't over- or under-scope the `no-restricted-imports` exemption.","+- **One enrollment engine, four callers**: FR-7 (add to section), FR-8","+  (remove from section), FR-9 (move-section transaction), and FR-10 (new","+  CORE course) all drive the same enroll/drop/reactivate semantics, and","+  FR-11's capacity guard plus FR-24's audit call must sit in front of/behind","+  all four. Design this as named, reusable service functions (e.g.","+  `enrollSectionCoreCourses`, `dropSectionCoreCourses`,","+  `checkCapacityOrThrow`), not duplicated per route — FR-9's single-transaction","+  requirement is the clearest signal these must already be composable,","+  transaction-safe units.","+- **FR-20's nav-grouping question is an explicit decision point**, not a","+  detail to quietly resolve — `handoff-to-architect.json` lists it as a","+  blocker. Record the chosen approach (add `group` to `NavItem` vs. flat","+  entries) in `system-design.md`'s Overview per Compliance Rule 5.","+- **NFR-7's auth-helper gap is also a carried-forward blocker** — FR-18's","+  `course-access.ts` must be designed against today's `requireRole.ts`","+  stand-in, not the not-yet-built `permissions.ts`/`requireUser()`. Surface","+  this explicitly in `system-design.md`'s Overview so `dev-planning` doesn't","+  schedule work against auth helpers that don't exist.","+- **Two independent app-level uniqueness rules have no DB backstop and need","+  an explicit concurrency decision**: FR-3's \"one homeroom teacher per","+  section per year\" and FR-14's \"section required iff kind=CORE\" are both","+  service-layer validations only (unlike NFR-2's DB-constrained rule) —","+  `arch-design-system` should say where each is checked and whether a race","+  (two concurrent section edits) is acceptable risk or needs a DB-level","+  constraint too.","+- **NFR-4's roster scrubbing must be enforced at the API layer (FR-23),","+  not just the FR-17 UI tab** — call this out explicitly in the","+  traceability table so the response-shape rule isn't treated as","+  presentation-only.","+- **FR-5 and FR-10 share one course-creation-enrolls-members code path** —","+  bulk (checked subjects) vs. single (one course) are two entry points into","+  the same underlying function, same pattern as the enrollment engine note","+  above."]}],"originalFile":"# Requirements Analysis Notes — ticket abcd-04\n\nSupporting document for `arch-design-system`. Not a primary phase output.\nSource: `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` (34 requirements — 24 FR + 10\nNFR, 97% confidence, no blockers per `handoff-to-architect.json`). Scope is\nthe Notifications & Email PRD: notification data model/registry, `notify()`\nservice, email templates, an email outbox + dispatcher worker, bell/popover\nUI, the `/notifications` page, preferences UI, and the admin email log.\n\nArchitectural concern buckets used below: **Data Model**, **Notification\nType Registry**, **Notification Service**, **Family Fan-out Helper**, **Email\nTemplates**, **Auth Email Integration**, **Email Dispatcher**, **Email\nTransport Config**, **Bell/Popover UI**, **Notifications Page**, **Retention\nJob**, **Preferences UI**, **Preferences API**, **Admin Email Log**, **REST\nAPI Surface**, **Navigation**, **Design System (MUI-only)**, **Security/\nAccess Control**, **Test Harness**, **Scope Discipline**.\n\n## Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| FR-1 | Data Model | `Notification`, `NotificationPreference`, `EmailOutbox` + `EmailStatus` enum. Foundation tables — every other FR reads or writes one of these; must land before any service/route work. |\n| FR-2 | Notification Type Registry | Typed registry at `src/server/notifications/types.ts` (label/description/category/defaultEmail/mandatoryInApp/roles), seeded with `account.invite`, `account.password_reset`, `system.test`. Single source of truth the service layer (FR-4), preferences UI (FR-18), and compile-time typing (FR-3) all key off. |\n| FR-3 | Notification Service | `notify()` must reject unknown type keys at compile time (TS literal/union against the registry) — a typing constraint on FR-4's signature, not a runtime check. |\n| FR-4 | Notification Service | Core `notify({ type, userIds, title, body?, link?, email? })` — skips archived/disabled users, resolves preferences (falling back to registry defaults), writes `Notification` rows and `EmailOutbox` rows as applicable. The central chokepoint everything else (FR-6, FR-9, FR-20, FR-24) calls into. |\n| FR-5 | Notification Service | Batching via `createMany` (≤3 round-trips per 1,000 users each for notifications/emails) + never-throw-to-caller (log failures) + \"call after your own transaction commits\" contract. Directly drives NFR-2's latency budget and the service's error-handling shape. |\n| FR-6 | Family Fan-out Helper | `notifyStudentAndParents({ studentIds, ...rest })` — expands student → student + linked parents, deduped, for later PRDs to reuse. Thin wrapper over FR-4 plus whatever parent-link read path already exists (brownfield reuse, not a new link model). |\n| FR-7 | Email Templates | `@react-email/components` templates under `src/server/email/templates/`, rendered to HTML + plain text, sharing a base layout (header/content/footer, preferences link, disclosure sentence). One shared layout component — avoid three independently-styled templates. |\n| FR-8 | Email Templates | Concrete templates: invite, password-reset, generic-notification, each with their specified fields/CTA. Three thin wrappers over FR-7's base layout. |\n| FR-9 | Auth Email Integration | Replace the PRD-01 `sendInvite()` stub (`src/server/services/users.ts:385-390`, currently log-only) with `sendAuthEmail()` that enqueues via `EmailOutbox`. A brownfield swap at an existing call site — must not change the stub's call signature/contract that PRD-01 callers depend on. |\n| FR-10 | Email Dispatcher | `dispatchEmails({ batchSize = 50 })` — `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` select of due PENDING rows, mark SENDING, send via Nodemailer, mark SENT or schedule retry. The other central chokepoint; must be designed as one module both the HTTP route (FR-12) and the worker loop (FR-12) call. |\n| FR-11 | Email Dispatcher | Retry backoff schedule (1/5/15/60/240 min across 5 attempts, FAILED after 5th) — state-machine detail inside FR-10's dispatch logic, also what FR-20's \"Retry now\" action resets. |\n| FR-12 | Email Dispatcher | Two callers of `dispatchEmails`: `POST /api/internal/email/dispatch` (bearer `CRON_SECRET`, ties to NFR-8) and `npm run worker:email` (15s loop, local/simple deployments). Same underlying function, two thin entry points — do not fork the dispatch logic between them. |\n| FR-13 | Email Transport Config | Env vars (`SMTP_*`, `EMAIL_FROM`, `CRON_SECRET`, `EMAIL_TRANSPORT` smtp\\|console). Console transport is a dev-mode Nodemailer transport swap, not a separate code path through the dispatcher. |\n| FR-14 | Bell/Popover UI | Live unread-count bell (MUI IconButton + Badge) replacing the static badge at `src/app/(app)/shell.tsx:141`; polls `GET /api/notifications/unread-count` every 30s, paused on `visibilitychange` hidden, \"9+\" cap. Read-only UI over FR-21's unread-count endpoint; polling pause/resume is a client-side concern only. |\n| FR-15 | Bell/Popover UI | Popover with 10 most recent notifications (unread styling, 2-line clamp, relative time), click marks-read + navigates; footer \"Mark all as read\" / \"View all\". Composes FR-21's list/read endpoints; no new server logic beyond what FR-16/FR-21 already define. |\n| FR-16 | Notifications Page | `/notifications`: paginated (20/page), All/Unread tabs, category filter, bulk mark-all-read, per-row read/unread toggle. Same list/read API surface as FR-15's popover — one list service backing both, not two. |\n| FR-17 | Retention Job | `purgeOldNotifications()` — delete notifications older than 180 days, invoked once/day from the dispatcher endpoint with in-memory last-run tracking (re-run after restart is acceptable). Piggybacks on FR-12's dispatch endpoint call site — not a separate cron/queue. |\n| FR-18 | Preferences UI | `/account/notifications`: MUI Table grouped by category, In-app/Email switches per visible type, mandatory in-app types shown disabled with tooltip. Pure read+toggle view over FR-2 (registry) + FR-1 (`NotificationPreference` rows). |\n| FR-19 | Preferences API | `PUT /api/me/notification-preferences` saves immediately, optimistic UI + rollback on error — client-side concern (FR-18) paired with the server validation in FR-22. |\n| FR-20 | Admin Email Log | `/admin/email-log`: table (created/recipient/subject/status/attempts/last-error), status filter, \"Retry now\" (resets attempts=0/status=PENDING/nextAttemptAt=now), \"Send test notification to me\" (calls `notify({ type: 'system.test', ... })`). Read/write surface directly over `EmailOutbox` (FR-1) and `notify()` (FR-4); \"Retry now\" must use the same reset semantics FR-11's backoff expects. |\n| FR-21 | REST API Surface | Full endpoint list (notifications CRUD-ish, preferences, dispatch, admin email-log, admin test) — to be finalized verbatim by `arch-api-contracts`, following PRD-00's envelope/pagination/error-code conventions. Every endpoint here must map 1:1 to a service already named in FR-4/FR-10/FR-16/FR-18/FR-20; no net-new business logic at the route layer. |\n| FR-22 | Preferences API | `PUT /api/me/notification-preferences` must reject `inApp=false` for `mandatoryInApp` types — server-side enforcement mirroring FR-18's disabled-switch UI (defense in depth, UI alone is not sufficient). |\n| FR-23 | Navigation | Sidebar nav entries for notifications page (+ admin email-log) in `src/config/navigation.ts`, following the existing `NavItem` convention (currently only Dashboard). MEDIUM confidence on exact entries — flag for `arch-design-system` to confirm naming/grouping. |\n| FR-24 | Data Model (seed) | Seed 3 `system.test` `Notification` rows (1 read, 2 unread) for two existing seeded users. Pure seed-data task, depends on FR-1's schema and FR-2's registry key existing first. |\n\n## Non-Functional Requirements\n\n| ID | Architectural Concern | Notes |\n|----|------------------------|-------|\n| NFR-1 | Design System (MUI-only) | Every visible surface (bell, popover, notifications page, preferences table, admin log) MUI-only, checked via MUI MCP, no banned libraries; email templates explicitly exempt (use `@react-email/components` instead). A lint-enforced boundary (`no-restricted-imports`) `arch-design-system` should scope per-directory so the email-template exemption doesn't leak into app UI code. |\n| NFR-2 | Notification Service | `notify()` for 1,000 recipients < 2s — directly validates FR-5's ≤3-round-trip batching design; a perf budget the service implementation must be benchmarked against. |\n| NFR-3 | Notifications Page / Bell | `GET /api/notifications/unread-count` < 50ms, backed by a `(userId, readAt, createdAt)` index — constrains the `Notification` table's indexing (FR-1) and the query shape of FR-14/FR-21's unread-count endpoint. |\n| NFR-4 | Email Dispatcher | No double-send under concurrent dispatchers (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`) + stuck-`SENDING`-for->10min recovery back to PENDING. A concurrency-safety invariant on FR-10's dispatch query — the stuck-row recovery is an extra step FR-10/FR-12 must schedule (e.g. at the top of each dispatch call), not an afterthought. |\n| NFR-5 | Security/Access Control | Notification `link` must be a validated relative path (`/`-prefixed, open-redirect prevention); email HTML must escape all user-provided text. Two related but distinct sanitization rules — one on `notify()`'s input (FR-4), one on the email template renderer (FR-7). |\n| NFR-6 | Email Templates | Every outbound email has HTML + plain-text parts + a `List-Unsubscribe` header pointing at the preferences page. Extends FR-7's base layout contract and FR-10's send step (header must be set at send time, not template-render time). |\n| NFR-7 | Security/Access Control | Server-side `requireUser()`/`requireRole()` on every route: own-notifications-only for user routes, ADMIN-only for email-log routes, `CRON_SECRET` bearer for the internal dispatch route. A per-route authorization matrix `arch-api-contracts` must encode explicitly against FR-21's endpoint list — hiding UI (FR-20's admin page) is never sufficient. |\n| NFR-8 | Email Dispatcher | Dispatch without correct `CRON_SECRET` bearer → 401 via the standard error envelope. The negative-path companion to FR-12/NFR-7's auth check on that one route. |\n| NFR-9 | Test Harness | Keep `lint`/`typecheck`/`test` green; add the unit/integration/Playwright tests listed in Test Requirements. Scopes exactly what `dev-planning` must schedule test-writing for. |\n| NFR-10 | Scope Discipline | No SMS/push, no websockets (bell polls only), no digest emails — bounds `arch-design-system`'s design the same way it bounds dev's; don't let FR-14's polling design grow into a push/websocket architecture. |\n\n## Cross-cutting notes for `arch-design-system`\n\n- **Two chokepoints to design as named, reusable modules, not inline\n  code**: `notify()` (FR-3/FR-4/FR-5/FR-6, in\n  `src/server/notifications/notify.ts`) and `dispatchEmails()` (FR-10/FR-11,\n  called identically by FR-12's HTTP route and worker loop). Nearly every\n  other FR is either a thin UI/read-view wrapper around these two, or a\n  template/config detail feeding them (FR-2 registry, FR-7/FR-8 templates,\n  FR-13 env config).\n- **One list/read service backs both the popover (FR-15) and the\n  `/notifications` page (FR-16)** — same pagination/filter/mark-read\n  surface, just different page sizes/UI chrome. Don't let `arch-design-system`\n  spec two separate query paths.\n- **Defense-in-depth pairs to call out explicitly in the traceability\n  table**: FR-18 (disabled switch) + FR-22 (server-side reject) for\n  mandatory in-app types; FR-9's brownfield stub swap must preserve\n  whatever call signature PRD-01's `sendInvite()` callers already use\n  (NFR-7-style reuse discipline, even though this PRD's NFRs don't\n  re-state it — see FR-9's own source note referencing\n  `src/server/services/users.ts:385-390`).\n- **NFR-4's stuck-`SENDING` recovery and FR-17's daily purge both need a\n  concrete trigger point** — likely \"run at the top of `dispatchEmails()`\n  calls, gated by in-memory last-run timestamps,\" per FR-17's own\n  acceptance of restart-driven re-runs. `arch-design-system` should pick\n  one scheduling mechanism for both rather than inventing two.\n- **FR-23's exact nav entries are MEDIUM confidence** (inferred from\n  layout conventions, not spelled out) — flag in `system-design.md`'s\n  traceability table so `dev-planning` knows the nav copy/grouping needs a\n  final decision, not just an endpoint contract.\n- **NFR-1's MUI-only rule needs a directory boundary, not a blanket rule**:\n  `src/server/email/templates/**` is explicitly exempt (React Email\n  components) while every app-UI path (bell, popover, `/notifications`,\n  `/account/notifications`, `/admin/email-log`) is MUI-only. Worth a single\n  sentence in `system-design.md`'s Overview so the ESLint config `dev`\n  writes doesn't over- or under-scope the `no-restricted-imports` exemption.\n","userModified":false}}
